    "lens": {
      "mechanics": "When money and credit spent grow faster than the quantity sold, prices rise. A bond’s yield after inflation is the return forgone by holding gold, which pays no interest.",
      "data": "Three forces set the context. <a href=\"https://bea.gov/sites/default/files/2026-11/pi1026.pdf\">October core PCE inflation was 3.3% year on year</a>, limiting room to ease. <a href=\"https://home.treasury.gov/resource-center/data-chart-center/interest-rates/TextView?type=daily_treasury_real_yield_curve&amp;field_tdr_date_value_month=202611\">The 10-year real yield rose to 2.79% on 27 Nov</a>, increasing gold’s opportunity cost. <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/T10YIE\">The 10-year breakeven rose to 2.15% on 27 Nov</a>, signalling slightly greater inflation compensation in bond prices. Gold still rebounded. Reuters reported bargain buying after the week’s low, a source of demand the yield comparison alone misses.",
      "unsustainable": "If interest consumes a rising share of public revenue, it squeezes the rest of the budget. Smaller deficits, debt restructuring, transfers and money creation distribute the burden in different ways.",
