      "cycle": "Debt remains high relative to annual receipts, with the ratio up from a year earlier on this measure. A household with a larger interest bill has less room for other spending, while a government that issues its own currency has different financing options. The revised growth estimate widened the negative yield-growth gaps, but a single quarter of annualised growth is a noisy guide to long-run debt service.",
      "asset_classes": "Positive after-inflation Treasury yields compete with gold. Cash earns short-term interest and loses buying power if prices rise faster. If debt pressure leads to easing that cuts real returns, gold can gain through that channel. Bonds depend on inflation and repayment terms, while stocks depend on productivity and profits outrunning financing costs."
